From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ludovic Henry <git@ludovic.dev>
Date: Tue, 17 Nov 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] test: arm a hard watchdog around the ctrl-c test

Upstream-Status: Inappropriate [riscv64-fleet-specific timing guard, not a
portability fix]

Real riscv64 CI (job 133351190021) shows the test suite passing 16/255
items, reaching test_abandoning_the_iterator_cancels_the_stream PASSED at
2026-11-16T16:22:31Z, then producing zero further log output for over 10
hours until the job's own 720-minute timeout silently killed it at
2026-11-17T02:31:30Z. The very next test in file order is
test_ctrl_c_interrupts_native_chunking.

The prior revision's own commit message timed this same _big_document()
chunk at "on the order of a minute" on these runners when it dropped the
upstream 5s completion budget in favor of retrying the SIGINT every 0.2s
until the channel-empty poll observes it. That retry assumes the channel
goes empty often enough during the run to observe a pending signal "soon".
Whether the 10-hour stall is that same race simply losing every round on a
sufficiently slow/loaded host, or something else entirely (e.g. a
background thread or channel left over from the previous test's
abandoned-iterator cleanup), is not established from the CI log alone -
there is no partial output at all to tell the two apart.

Either way, nothing should be able to silently consume the rest of the
job's timeout budget. Arm a hard watchdog via the stdlib faulthandler
module, well beyond any plausible legitimate duration for this document on
this fleet, that dumps every thread's real stack and aborts the process
outright if this test is still running when it fires. A genuine hang then
produces an immediate, actionable failure (with a real thread-state dump
to diagnose from) instead of silently eating a 12-hour runner slot.
---
 crates/docling-py/tests/test_chunking.py | 42 ++++++++++++++++++++++++------
 1 file changed, 34 insertions(+), 8 deletions(-)

diff --git a/crates/docling-py/tests/test_chunking.py b/crates/docling-py/tests/test_chunking.py
index 79c37a3..27b2038 100644
--- a/crates/docling-py/tests/test_chunking.py
+++ b/crates/docling-py/tests/test_chunking.py
@@ -172,45 +172,65 @@ def test_abandoning_the_iterator_cancels_the_stream():
 def test_ctrl_c_interrupts_native_chunking():
     # A SIGINT arriving while the Rust side is chunking must raise
     # KeyboardInterrupt promptly, not stall until the native call returns.
+    import faulthandler
     import signal
     import threading
     import time

     doc = _big_document()

-    # __next__ only checks for a pending signal when its 100ms channel poll
-    # times out empty; on a slow/few-core host the channel can stay non-empty
-    # for the whole run, so a single fixed-delay SIGINT can be missed.
-    # Re-issue it until the call is interrupted, instead of once.
-    stop = threading.Event()
-
-    def resignal():
-        while not stop.wait(0.2):
-            signal.raise_signal(signal.SIGINT)
-
-    timer = threading.Timer(0.5, resignal)
-    timer.daemon = True
-    timer.start()
-    try:
-        with pytest.raises(KeyboardInterrupt):
-            list(HybridChunker(tokenizer=str(TOKENIZER), max_tokens=64).chunk(doc))
-    finally:
-        # resignal() can commit to one more raise_signal() after observing
-        # `stop` only on its next 0.2s poll, so a SIGINT can still be pending
-        # delivery to this thread right here - including to the very next
-        # statement run, whichever one that is. A real riscv64 run proved
-        # that the hard way: wrapping only timer.join() in retry-on-
-        # KeyboardInterrupt still let a trailing signal escape uncaught from
-        # stop.set() itself, since Python can act on a pending signal at any
-        # bytecode boundary, not only inside the call being retried. Put
-        # every statement this cleanup needs inside the retried block, not
-        # just the one expected to block, so there is no statement left
-        # outside it for a trailing signal to hit.
-        while True:
-            try:
-                stop.set()
-                timer.join()
-            except KeyboardInterrupt:
-                continue
-            else:
-                break
+    # The retry loop below assumes the channel goes empty often enough during
+    # the run to observe a pending signal "soon". A real riscv64 CI run (job
+    # 133351190021) instead stalled here for over 10 hours - far beyond the
+    # "on the order of a minute" this same document's chunking was timed at
+    # when that retry loop was written - with zero log output, until the
+    # job's own 720-minute timeout silently killed it. Whether that is this
+    # same race simply losing every single round on a sufficiently
+    # slow/loaded host, or something else (e.g. a channel/thread left over
+    # from the previous test's abandoned-iterator cleanup) isn't established
+    # from CI logs alone. Either way, nothing should be able to silently eat
+    # the rest of the job's timeout budget: arm a hard watchdog, well beyond
+    # any plausible legitimate duration for this document on this fleet, that
+    # dumps every thread's real stack and aborts the process outright if this
+    # test is still running when it fires - turning a silent multi-hour stall
+    # into an immediate, diagnosable failure.
+    faulthandler.dump_traceback_later(1200, exit=True)
+    try:
+        # __next__ only checks for a pending signal when its 100ms channel poll
+        # times out empty; on a slow/few-core host the channel can stay non-empty
+        # for the whole run, so a single fixed-delay SIGINT can be missed.
+        # Re-issue it until the call is interrupted, instead of once.
+        stop = threading.Event()
+
+        def resignal():
+            while not stop.wait(0.2):
+                signal.raise_signal(signal.SIGINT)
+
+        timer = threading.Timer(0.5, resignal)
+        timer.daemon = True
+        timer.start()
+        try:
+            with pytest.raises(KeyboardInterrupt):
+                list(HybridChunker(tokenizer=str(TOKENIZER), max_tokens=64).chunk(doc))
+        finally:
+            # resignal() can commit to one more raise_signal() after observing
+            # `stop` only on its next 0.2s poll, so a SIGINT can still be pending
+            # delivery to this thread right here - including to the very next
+            # statement run, whichever one that is. A real riscv64 run proved
+            # that the hard way: wrapping only timer.join() in retry-on-
+            # KeyboardInterrupt still let a trailing signal escape uncaught from
+            # stop.set() itself, since Python can act on a pending signal at any
+            # bytecode boundary, not only inside the call being retried. Put
+            # every statement this cleanup needs inside the retried block, not
+            # just the one expected to block, so there is no statement left
+            # outside it for a trailing signal to hit.
+            while True:
+                try:
+                    stop.set()
+                    timer.join()
+                except KeyboardInterrupt:
+                    continue
+                else:
+                    break
+    finally:
+        faulthandler.cancel_dump_traceback_later()
--
2.43.0
